# Accessibility Widget — Access Control & Configuration PRD

## Scope

The Wcagify accessibility widget is a dependency-free browser overlay maintained in a
separate repo (`wcagify-frontend.ai`, source at `widget/wcagify.js`, shipped as
`public/wcagify.min.js`). It is embedded on a customer's own website with a single
`<script>` tag and today reads all of its settings from local `data-*` attributes or a
`window.WcagifyConfig` global.

This module adds the server side of that product to the platform backend:

- Internal staff grant access from Django Admin, and companies self-serve a 7-day
  trial from the dashboard.
- **A subscription licenses exactly one domain.** A company with several websites
  holds several subscriptions, each with its own plan, keys, and configuration.
- The widget calls a public bootstrap endpoint with its key, is authorized against
  the requesting origin, and receives that company's published configuration.
- The company configures appearance, features, keys, and domains from a new page in
  the platform dashboard.
- Access is sold as a recurring subscription: 7-day trials, five plan tiers priced by
  site size, monthly and annual billing, automatic renewal, upgrades and downgrades,
  grace periods, page-limit handling, and invoicing.

A payment gateway is **not** part of the first delivery. Everything in §2 is built
against a provider interface with a manual/credit-based implementation, so that
adding a gateway later is a new implementation of that interface rather than a
redesign. The columns a gateway needs exist from the first migration.

Out of scope for this document: the widget-side `fetch` and config merge, which are
implemented in `wcagify-frontend.ai`. The contract that change must satisfy is
specified in §8.

## Placement

New Django app `apps.widget` rather than an extension of `apps.core`. This is a
distinct product surface with its own admin, its own public unauthenticated endpoint,
and its own migration cadence; folding it into `apps.core` (already carrying company,
user, project, billing, and pricing) buys nothing. Tables stay in the `platform`
Postgres schema, matching the existing `db_table = 'platform"."…'` convention.

```
platform/backend/apps/widget/
    models.py       services.py      admin.py
    keys.py         migrations/      tests/
platform/backend/apps/api/
    widget_public.py      # unauthenticated bootstrap endpoint
    widget_console.py     # authenticated company-facing CRUD
platform/frontend/
    widget.html           js/widget.js
```

`'apps.widget'` is added to `INSTALLED_APPS` in `config/settings.py`.

## Feature Breakdown

### 1. Data Model

The unit of sale is **one domain**. A company with three websites holds three
subscriptions, each with its own plan, billing period, keys, and configuration. The
split below is deliberate: the grant, the identity, and the configuration change at
different rates and are edited by different actors.

#### `WidgetSubscription` — the grant (one per domain)

| Field | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `company` | `FK(Company, related_name='widget_subscriptions')` | **not** one-to-one — a company holds many |
| `hostname` | `CharField(255)` | the single licensed domain, normalized; see below |
| `plan` | `FK(WidgetPlan, PROTECT)` | entitlements come from here, never from the subscription |
| `status` | see §2.2 lifecycle | `TRIALING`, `ACTIVE`, `PAST_DUE`, `SUSPENDED`, `CANCELED`, `EXPIRED` |
| `billing_interval` | `MONTHLY` \| `ANNUAL` | |
| `granted_by` | `FK(PlatformUser, SET_NULL)` | audit: which staff member granted |
| `granted_at`, `suspended_reason` | | |
| `trial_start`, `trial_end` | DateTimeField(null) | |
| `current_period_start`, `current_period_end` | DateTimeField | |
| `auto_renew` | Boolean, default `True` | |
| `cancel_at_period_end` | Boolean, default `False` | |
| `grace_until` | DateTimeField(null) | set on renewal failure |
| `renewal_attempts` | PositiveInteger, default 0 | |
| `verification_status`, `verification_token`, `verified_at` | | DNS TXT `wcagify-verify=<token>` |
| `provider`, `provider_customer_id`, `provider_subscription_id`, `provider_payment_method_id` | CharField(blank) | unused until a gateway exists |
| `max_pages_override` | null | per-subscription exception to the plan limit |
| `created_at`, `updated_at` | | |

**Hostname normalization** happens in `save()` via a shared `normalize_hostname()`
helper: lowercased, punycode, scheme/port/path/credentials stripped. `HTTPS://Shop.ACME.com:8443/`
and `shop.acme.com` must never become two rows.

**Subdomains are not included.** `acme.com` and `blog.acme.com` are separate products
requiring separate subscriptions, and the bootstrap host match is exact (§3).

One carve-out is needed, and it is not optional: **`www.` is not a subdomain for this
purpose.** Practically every site on the internet serves both `acme.com` and
`www.acme.com`, frequently on the same pages, and a customer who bought "acme.com"
and finds the widget dead on half their URLs will read it as a broken product rather
than a licensing boundary. Normalization therefore strips a leading `www.`, and the
canonical form covers both. Every other label is a distinct domain.

**Global uniqueness.** A partial unique index on `hostname` across serving statuses
(`TRIALING`, `ACTIVE`, `PAST_DUE`) — not scoped to company. Two companies must not
hold live subscriptions for the same domain simultaneously; that is either a mistake
or someone claiming a domain they do not control. Lapsed and canceled rows are
excluded so a domain can be re-sold later.

Access is the presence of a row in a serving status. No row for a host means the
bootstrap endpoint denies; no row at all for a company means the dashboard page is
hidden.

Note what is **not** on this model: page limits and feature flags live on
`WidgetPlan`, with a nullable override here. Copying entitlements onto the
subscription at grant time would mean a plan change never reaches existing
subscribers, which is the most common way this kind of model goes wrong.

#### `WidgetDomainTrial` — the one-trial-per-domain ledger

| Field | Notes |
|---|---|
| `registrable_domain` | CharField(255), **unique** — eTLD+1, e.g. `acme.com` |
| `first_claimed_hostname` | the exact host the trial was started on |
| `company` | `FK(Company, SET_NULL)` — who claimed it |
| `subscription` | `FK(WidgetSubscription, SET_NULL)` |
| `claimed_at`, `claimed_by_ip` | |

A row is written when a trial starts and is **never deleted** — not when the trial
expires, not when the subscription is canceled, not when the company is deleted.
Deleting it would reopen the loophole it exists to close.

Keyed on the **registrable domain (eTLD+1), not the exact hostname**. If the ledger
keyed on the hostname, `acme.com`, `www2.acme.com`, and `shop.acme.com` would each
grant a fresh 7-day trial, which is a trivially discoverable way to run the product
free forever. Deriving eTLD+1 needs the Public Suffix List (`publicsuffix2`), so that
`acme.co.uk` is treated correctly rather than collapsing every `.co.uk` site into one
trial. Add the dependency; hand-rolling "last two labels" is wrong for the ~9 000
multi-label suffixes.

Two consequences to state plainly, because they are real and staff will meet them:

- A company that legitimately runs `acme.com` and `shop.acme.com` as separate
  products gets **one** trial across both. That is the intended trade.
- A domain that changes hands to a genuinely different owner will find its trial
  already spent. There is no automatic remedy: staff can grant a trial from admin,
  which records an override reason on the ledger row.

#### `WidgetKey` — the public identifier

| Field | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | `UUIDField(primary_key=True, default=uuid4)` | |
| `subscription` | `FK(WidgetSubscription, related_name='keys')` | |
| `key` | `CharField(48, unique=True, db_index=True)` | |
| `label` | CharField | e.g. `production`, `staging` |
| `status` | `ACTIVE` \| `REVOKED` | |
| `last_seen_at` | DateTimeField(null) | updated at most hourly, see §6 |
| `created_by`, `created_at`, `revoked_at` | | |

Format: `wcg_` followed by 32 characters of Crockford base32 — alphanumeric, and
excluding `I`, `L`, `O`, `U` so a key read aloud or retyped by a customer is
unambiguous. Roughly 100 bits of entropy.

```python
ALPHABET = '0123456789ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTVWXYZ'

def generate_widget_key() -> str:
    return 'wcg_' + ''.join(secrets.choice(ALPHABET) for _ in range(32))
```

**The key is stored in plaintext and indexed, not hashed.** It ships inside a
`<script>` tag on a public web page, so it is a tenant identifier, not a secret.
Hashing it — the reflex carried over from API tokens — would buy no real protection
while costing the ability to display it in the dashboard and to resolve it in one
indexed lookup. The security boundary is origin binding (§3), not key secrecy. This
reasoning belongs in the model docstring so it is not "fixed" later.

Allowing several keys per subscription is what makes rotation non-breaking: issue a
new key, let the customer swap the script tag, then revoke the old one.

#### Domain scope — no separate table

Because a subscription *is* a domain, there is no `WidgetDomain` table: `hostname`,
`verification_status`, `verification_token`, and `verified_at` are columns on
`WidgetSubscription`. A one-row child table would add a join to the hottest lookup in
the system for no expressiveness.

`localhost` and `127.0.0.1` are accepted only while the subscription is `TRIALING`,
or behind an explicit `allow_localhost` flag, so a developer can integrate before
going live.

Domain verification is not required for v1 — a staff member or a self-serve signup
naming the domain is sufficient — but the columns exist from the first migration,
because retrofitting verification onto live subscriptions is disruptive. It matters
more here than in the earlier multi-domain design: with global hostname uniqueness
(§1), an unverified claim on a domain someone else owns blocks the rightful owner
from subscribing.

#### `WidgetConfiguration` — the draft config (one per subscription)

Typed columns for everything the widget documents, so admin and dashboard get real
validation instead of a free-form blob:

| Field | Notes |
|---|---|
| `subscription` | `OneToOneField(WidgetSubscription)` |
| `accent_color` | `CharField(7)`, hex-color validator |
| `position` | choices: `bottom-right`, `bottom-left`, `top-right`, `top-left` |
| `icon_url` | URLField, blank |
| `root_selector`, `main_selector`, `storage_key` | CharField, blank |
| `dictionary_endpoint` | URLField, blank |
| `auto_init` | Boolean, default `True` |
| `enabled_profiles` | JSONField(list) — subset of the widget's built-in profiles |
| `enabled_tools` | JSONField(list) — reader, dictionary, voice, mousegrid, structure |
| `glossary` | JSONField(dict) — custom Dictionary entries |
| `extra` | JSONField(dict) — forward-compatibility escape hatch |
| `current_version` | PositiveInteger, default 0 |
| `updated_by`, `updated_at` | |

#### `WidgetConfigVersion` — immutable published snapshots

`id` (UUID), `subscription` (FK), `version` (PositiveInteger),
`payload` (JSONField), `published_by` (FK `PlatformUser`), `published_at`,
`etag` (CharField 64). `UniqueConstraint(subscription, version)`; `save()` raises
`ValidationError` on any update.

This follows the immutability pattern already established by `JobPricingSnapshot`
and `CurrencyConversionQuote` in `apps/core/models.py`. It provides three things:
draft-versus-live separation, so dashboard edits do not reach production until
Publish; rollback; and a stable `etag` for HTTP caching. `payload` is the exact JSON
the widget receives, computed once at publish time and never at request time.

### 2. Plans, Subscription Lifecycle & Billing

#### 2.1 Plan catalog

Five tiers, metered on **site size in pages**. Pageviews and visits are explicitly
unlimited on every tier — traffic is never metered, priced, or capped.

| `code` | Name | `rank` | Monthly | Annual | `max_pages` | Trial | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `small` | Small | 10 | $39 | $390 | 100 | 7 days | |
| `medium` | Medium | 20 | $49 | $490 | 1 000 | 7 days | marked POPULAR on the pricing page |
| `large` | Large | 30 | $109 | $1 090 | 10 000 | 7 days | |
| `huge` | Huge | 40 | $169 | $1 690 | 100 000 | 7 days | |
| `managed` | Managed | 50 | quote | quote | negotiated | — | quote-only; adds expert remediation |

Every price is **per domain, per period**. All amounts are USD at launch.

The annual figures work out to ten months for twelve on every tier, but that
relationship is a pricing decision, not a rule — `annual_price` is stored as its own
absolute column and read directly. Nothing in the code derives one price from the
other or from a discount percentage, so the two can diverge on any tier at any time
without a code change.

Every self-serve tier ships the identical widget — the auto AI-powered overlay with
its full profile and tool set. **Price is a function of site size only, not of
features.** This is a deliberate consequence of the pricing design and it simplifies
a great deal: there is no per-tier feature matrix to maintain, and the entitlement
filter in §2.5 has almost nothing to filter for tiers `small` through `huge`.

`managed` is the exception: it adds human, fully customizable remediation, which is
the existing document-remediation pipeline in `cluster/` sold as a service alongside
the widget. It has no self-serve price and no trial — it is a sales conversation.

**Trials.** Seven days, no payment method required, on every self-serve tier. A trial
auto-expires: at `trial_end` the subscription moves to `EXPIRED` and the widget stops
serving, with no charge and no card on file. **One trial per registrable domain, ever**
— enforced by the `WidgetDomainTrial` ledger in §1, checked before any trial starts,
regardless of which company asks.

**Currency.** USD only at launch; see §2.7 for what adding a second currency requires.

#### `WidgetPlan` — the catalog

| Field | Notes |
|---|---|
| `id` | UUID |
| `code` | CharField, unique, stable (`small`, `medium`, `large`, `huge`, `managed`) |
| `name`, `description` | display; `description` is the "Auto AI-powered widget for…" line |
| `rank` | PositiveInteger — **defines upgrade vs downgrade**; never inferred from price |
| `currency` | `CURRENCY_CHOICES` from `apps.core.models` |
| `monthly_price`, `annual_price` | Decimal(12,2), null when `is_quote_only`; both absolute, neither derived from the other |
| `trial_days` | PositiveInteger, 7 for self-serve, 0 for `managed` |
| `max_pages` | PositiveInteger — **the metered dimension**; null = negotiated |
| `page_limit_policy` | `NOTIFY` \| `BLOCK` — see §2.6; `NOTIFY` for every paid tier |
| `is_quote_only` | Boolean — no self-serve checkout, renders "Ask for a quote" |
| `is_popular` | Boolean — drives the POPULAR badge; presentation belongs with the plan |
| `grace_days` | PositiveInteger |
| `entitlements` | JSONField — allowed profiles, allowed tools, branding flags, feature booleans |
| `is_public` | Boolean — hide private/legacy plans from the pricing page |
| `effective_from`, `effective_to` | plans are versioned, not edited in place |

There is deliberately **no `monthly_load_quota`, `overage_unit_loads`, or
`overage_unit_price`.** "Unlimited pageviews & visits" is a headline promise on every
card; a schema that can express a traffic charge is a schema someone will eventually
wire up. Leaving the columns out makes the promise structural.

Seed the catalog as a data migration plus a `seed_widget_plans` management command,
so a fresh environment and the test suite get the same five rows.

Effective dating mirrors `PricingPlan` in `apps/core/models.py`. A price change
creates a new row; existing subscriptions keep their `PROTECT`-ed FK to the old one
until they are explicitly migrated. Editing a live plan's price in place would
silently reprice every existing customer at their next renewal.

Effective dating mirrors `PricingPlan` in `apps/core/models.py`. A price change
creates a new row; existing subscriptions keep their `PROTECT`-ed FK to the old one
until they are explicitly migrated. Editing a live plan's price in place would
silently reprice every existing customer at their next renewal.

#### `WidgetSubscriptionChange` — immutable lifecycle audit

`subscription`, `from_plan`, `to_plan`, `from_interval`, `to_interval`,
`change_type` (`TRIAL_START`, `TRIAL_CONVERT`, `UPGRADE`, `DOWNGRADE`,
`INTERVAL_CHANGE`, `RENEW`, `CANCEL`, `REACTIVATE`), `scheduled_for`, `effective_at`,
`proration_amount`, `currency`, `initiated_by` (`FK PlatformUser`, null for system),
`reason`, `created_at`. Immutable on save, following `JobPricingSnapshot`.

This table is what makes "why is this customer on this price?" answerable months
later, and it is also the scheduling mechanism for deferred downgrades.

#### `WidgetUsagePeriod` — measured site size per billing period

`subscription`, `period_start`, `period_end`, `pages_seen` (distinct),
`max_pages_applied`, `peak_pages_seen`, `over_limit_since` (null),
`notified_80_at`, `notified_100_at`, `finalized_at`.
`UniqueConstraint(subscription, period_start)`. Populated by the nightly rollup in
§6 and finalized at period close.

Note this table carries **no money columns**. Under this pricing, usage never changes
an invoice amount — it only determines whether the customer is on the right tier.
That is a much weaker coupling than usage-based billing, and it is worth keeping the
schema honest about it.

#### `WidgetPageObservation` — distinct page tracking

`subscription`, `period_start`, `path_hash` (CharField 64, indexed), `first_seen_at`,
`last_seen_at`. `UniqueConstraint(subscription, period_start, path_hash)`.

`path_hash` is `sha256(subscription_id || normalized_path)` — a salted hash of the
**path only**, never the query string, never a full URL, never stored in the clear.
This is enough to count distinct pages and useless for reconstructing a customer's
site map or a visitor's browsing. See §6 for how rows get here without a write per
request, and §2.6 for why counting distinct pages cannot use the sampling that the
rest of the telemetry does.

#### 2.2 Lifecycle

```
                 ┌──────────┐  trial_end reached — auto-expires, no card involved
     claim ─────▶│ TRIALING │──────────────────────────────▶ EXPIRED
                 └────┬─────┘
                      │ trial converts / paid
                      ▼
                 ┌──────────┐  renewal charge fails
                 │  ACTIVE  │─────────────────▶ PAST_DUE
                 └────┬─────┘                      │
   cancel_at_period_end│                           │ grace_days elapse
                      ▼                            ▼
                  CANCELED                     SUSPENDED
                                                   │ payment received
                                                   └────────▶ ACTIVE
```

**Serving statuses** — the bootstrap endpoint returns config for `TRIALING`,
`ACTIVE`, and `PAST_DUE`. It denies for `SUSPENDED`, `CANCELED`, and `EXPIRED`.

`PAST_DUE` deliberately keeps serving. The widget is running on the customer's live
public website; yanking it the moment a card fails is a visible, user-facing failure
on a site the platform does not own, over what is usually a transient billing
problem. The grace period is the whole point — dunning happens by email while the
widget keeps working, and only a fully lapsed subscription stops serving.

#### 2.3 Renewal

A management command `renew_widget_subscriptions`, run daily (the repo already uses
`apps/*/management` commands), handling:

1. `TRIALING` where `trial_end <= now` → `EXPIRED`, and invalidate the config cache
   so the widget stops serving. No card is held, so there is nothing to attempt: a
   trial converts only when the customer explicitly buys during the 7 days. The
   `WidgetDomainTrial` row stays behind permanently.
2. `ACTIVE` where `current_period_end <= now`:
   - `cancel_at_period_end` → `CANCELED`.
   - apply any `WidgetSubscriptionChange` with `scheduled_for <= now` (deferred
     downgrades) **before** pricing the new period.
   - close `WidgetUsagePeriod`, compute overage, issue the invoice, attempt payment.
   - success → advance the period, reset `renewal_attempts`; failure → `PAST_DUE`
     with `grace_until = now + plan.grace_days`.
3. `PAST_DUE` where `grace_until <= now` → `SUSPENDED`, and invalidate the widget
   config cache so serving stops promptly.

Requirements on this command: it must be **idempotent and safely re-runnable** (a
failed nightly run that gets retried must not double-charge), it must take a
`select_for_update` row lock per subscription, and each charge must carry a
deterministic idempotency key — `widget-renew:{subscription_id}:{period_start}` —
reusing the `Invoice.idempotency_key` constraint that already exists in
`apps/core/models.py`.

#### 2.4 Upgrades and downgrades

Direction is determined by `WidgetPlan.rank`, never by comparing prices — a monthly
plan and an annual plan of different tiers do not compare meaningfully by amount.

- **Upgrade** takes effect immediately. Charge a proration for the remainder of the
  current period, computed as
  `(new_period_rate - old_period_rate) * remaining_days / period_days`, rounded to
  the account currency's minor unit. Entitlements widen at once.
- **Downgrade** is scheduled for `current_period_end` via a
  `WidgetSubscriptionChange` row. No refund is issued for the unused richer tier;
  the customer keeps what they paid for until the period ends. This must be stated
  in the dashboard UI at the moment of the click.
- **Interval change** (monthly → annual) follows the upgrade path; annual → monthly
  follows the downgrade path.

**Conflicts on downgrade.** Because tiers differ almost entirely by `max_pages`, the
common conflict is a customer downgrading below their measured site size. Since page
limits are `NOTIFY` (§2.6), this is a warning and not a block: the dashboard states
"your site measured 4 200 pages last period; Medium covers 1 000" and requires an
explicit confirmation. The widget keeps serving either way.

There is no domain-count conflict to handle — one subscription covers exactly one
domain on every tier, so a plan change can never orphan a live site. This is a real
simplification the per-domain model buys.

Downgrading out of `managed` also ends the expert-remediation engagement; that path
routes to sales rather than executing self-serve.

#### 2.5 Entitlement enforcement — apply at read, not at publish

The published `WidgetConfigVersion.payload` is what the customer authored. The plan
entitlement filter is applied **when the bootstrap response is assembled**, not baked
into the payload at publish time.

This ordering is load-bearing. If entitlements were applied at publish, a customer
who downgrades would keep serving premium features until the next time they happened
to press Publish — which might be never. Applying at read means a plan change,
suspension, or scheduled downgrade takes effect on the next cache expiry with no
customer action. The filter is cheap and its result is what gets cached, so the
per-request cost stays zero.

The cache key therefore includes the plan and subscription status generation
counters, and any subscription state transition bumps them (§6).

Under the catalog in §2.1 this filter is close to a no-op for `small` through `huge`,
since those tiers differ only in `max_pages` and all ship the same widget. Keep the
mechanism anyway — it is what makes suspension, cancellation, and trial expiry take
effect, and it is the seam any future feature-gated tier will need.

#### 2.6 Page limits — measurement and enforcement

The metered dimension is **distinct pages of the customer's site on which the widget
loads**, counted per billing period against `plan.max_pages`. Traffic is not metered.

**Measurement.** A page is counted when the widget bootstraps on a URL path not yet
seen this period. The path comes from the `Referer` header — the same header already
used for origin fallback — normalized as: path only, query string and fragment
discarded, trailing slash stripped, lowercased, then salted-hashed. Raw paths are
never stored (§1, `WidgetPageObservation`).

**This count cannot be sampled.** Distinct-cardinality and sampling are incompatible:
a 1-in-50 sample of page loads systematically undercounts the long tail of rarely
visited pages, which on a large site is most of the pages. The 1-in-50 rule in §6
applies to the *event* telemetry only. Distinct pages are counted on every request
through a Redis HyperLogLog plus a small set-membership check — never Postgres —
and flushed to `WidgetPageObservation` and `WidgetUsagePeriod.pages_seen` by the
nightly rollup. HLL gives ~0.8% error at negligible memory, which is far inside the
10× gaps between tiers.

**Enforcement — `page_limit_policy = NOTIFY` on every paid tier.** Exceeding the page
limit must **not** stop the widget from serving. This is an accessibility overlay on
a live public website; switching it off mid-period because the customer's CMS
published more articles than their tier allows removes an accessibility affordance
from real disabled users, over a billing threshold. It also converts a routine
upsell into an outage the customer discovers from complaints.

Instead, crossing the limit:

- stamps `over_limit_since` and keeps serving every page,
- emails the company `notification_email` at 80% and again at 100%,
- shows a persistent upgrade prompt in the dashboard naming the measured page count
  and the tier that covers it,
- surfaces the subscription in an admin queue for sales follow-up.

`BLOCK` exists in the schema for the trial only, and even there it is not the default.

**One consequence to accept:** with `NOTIFY`, nothing technically forces an upgrade,
so tier compliance is a commercial process rather than an enforced one. That is the
correct trade for this product, but it should be a decision made knowingly — the
alternative degrades a disabled visitor's experience to collect $60/month.

#### 2.7 Invoicing

**Reuse `apps.core.Invoice` rather than creating a parallel invoice table.** A
company that buys both document remediation and the widget should see one billing
history with one numbering sequence, and the existing model already enforces money
immutability, the idempotency constraint, and the currency snapshot.

Two additions:

- `Invoice.kind` — `JOB_USAGE` \| `WIDGET_SUBSCRIPTION` (default `JOB_USAGE` so
  existing rows migrate cleanly).
- `WidgetInvoiceLine` — `invoice` (FK), `subscription` (FK), `line_type`
  (`SUBSCRIPTION` \| `OVERAGE` \| `PRORATION` \| `CREDIT`), `description`,
  `quantity`, `unit_amount`, `amount`, `period_start`, `period_end`. Immutable.
  Line amounts must sum to `Invoice.amount`; assert this in the invoice service.

**Currency.** `prds/billing-currency-invariant.md` applies here without exception.
Subscription amounts are denominated in `WidgetPlan.currency`. If that differs from
the company's `BillingAccount.currency`, a persisted `CurrencyConversionQuote` for
that exact pair is required and referenced from the subscription — there is no
implicit conversion and no relabelling. The simplest way to avoid this entirely is to
publish each plan in every currency offered, and that is the recommended path.

#### 2.8 Payment provider boundary

`apps/widget/services/payments.py` defines the interface the renewal command calls:

```python
class PaymentProvider(Protocol):
    def charge(self, subscription, amount, currency, idempotency_key) -> ChargeResult: ...
    def refund(self, charge_ref, amount, idempotency_key) -> ChargeResult: ...
    def has_payment_method(self, subscription) -> bool: ...
```

Two implementations at launch:

- `CreditBalanceProvider` — deducts from the company's existing
  `BillingAccount.remaining_credit` using the same atomic, currency-guarded SQL
  predicate the worker already uses for job charges. This makes the whole
  subscription system fully functional today with no gateway.
- `ManualInvoiceProvider` — issues the invoice as `PENDING` and leaves it for staff
  to mark paid from admin. Correct for enterprise deals.

Selected per subscription by the `provider` column. A gateway becomes a third
implementation plus a webhook endpoint; no lifecycle, invoicing, or entitlement code
changes.

`managed` subscriptions use `ManualInvoiceProvider` by definition — quote-only plans
have no self-serve charge path.

#### 2.9 The Managed tier and quote requests

`managed` is the only `is_quote_only` plan. It bundles the widget with expert
remediation, which is the pipeline this repo already runs (`cluster/`), so it is the
one tier where the widget product and the document product meet.

Implications that fall out of "Ask for a quote":

- `WidgetQuoteRequest` — `company` (nullable: the request may precede signup),
  `contact_name`, `contact_email`, `site_url`, `estimated_pages`, `message`,
  `status` (`NEW`, `CONTACTED`, `WON`, `LOST`), `assigned_to`, `created_at`. Reachable
  from the public pricing page and from the dashboard's plan tab.
- No self-serve path may ever put a company onto `managed`. Staff assign it from
  admin after a deal, with negotiated `max_pages` and price captured
  as a company-specific non-public `WidgetPlan` row (`is_public = False`) — which is
  exactly what plan versioning already supports, so no bespoke "custom price" column
  is needed on the subscription.
- The dashboard renders `managed` as "Ask for a quote" with a contact form, never a
  checkout button, and `POST /api/widget/subscription/change-plan/` rejects a
  `plan_code` whose plan is `is_quote_only`.

### 3. Bootstrap Endpoint (public)

`GET /api/widget/bootstrap/` — `AllowAny`, no authentication classes, no cookies.

Request: `?key=wcg_XXXX`.

**Authorization uses the `Origin` header, not a client-supplied domain parameter.**
Any domain passed in the query string is attacker-controlled: a scraper can lift a
key from a customer's public page and claim any origin it likes. `Origin` is set by
the browser and cannot be forged by page JavaScript. A `domain` parameter may be
accepted for diagnostics but must not affect the decision.

```python
def resolve_request_host(request) -> str | None:
    origin = request.headers.get('Origin')
    if origin and origin != 'null':
        return normalize_hostname(urlparse(origin).hostname)
    referer = request.headers.get('Referer')
    if referer:
        return normalize_hostname(urlparse(referer).hostname)
    return None      # deny: not a browser page load
```

Resolution order in `services.resolve_bootstrap()`, each step short-circuiting to a
typed denial:

| Check | Denial `reason` |
|---|---|
| Key exists and `status=ACTIVE` | `unknown_key` |
| Subscription is in a serving status — `TRIALING`, `ACTIVE`, `PAST_DUE` (§2.2) | `suspended` / `canceled` |
| Trial not lapsed | `trial_expired` |
| Host equals `subscription.hostname` — **exact match**, `www.` already normalized away (§1) | `domain_not_allowed` |
| Page limit, **only** when `plan.page_limit_policy = BLOCK` (§2.6) | `page_limit_exceeded` |
| Plan entitlement filter applied to the payload (§2.5) | — narrows config, never denies |

On every paid tier the page-limit row never fires: `page_limit_policy` is `NOTIFY`,
and exceeding the tier is handled commercially rather than by refusing to serve.
Traffic volume is never a denial reason at all — pageviews are unlimited by design.

`200` response, shaped to drop straight into `window.WcagifyConfig` so the widget can
call `Wcagify.init(response.config)`:

```json
{
  "ok": true,
  "version": 7,
  "config": {
    "accentColor": "#0f766e",
    "position": "bottom-left",
    "iconUrl": "https://acme.com/a11y.svg",
    "rootSelector": "#app",
    "mainSelector": "main",
    "storageKey": "acme.a11y.prefs",
    "dictionaryEndpoint": null,
    "profiles": ["dyslexia", "adhd", "colorBlindness"],
    "tools": ["reader", "dictionary", "structure"],
    "glossary": {}
  }
}
```

Every denial returns `403` with `{"ok": false, "reason": "domain_not_allowed"}` —
one status code, so the widget needs exactly one branch: anything other than `200`
means do not render, warn once, never retry. A bad key must degrade to the widget
being absent, never to a JavaScript error on a customer's site.

Response headers on `200`:

```
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: <echoed request origin>
Vary: Origin
Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
ETag: "<WidgetConfigVersion.etag>"
```

`If-None-Match` is honored and returns `304`.

**CORS conflict.** `config/settings.py` sets `CORS_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS = True` with an
explicit `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` allowlist for the dashboard. Enabling
`CORS_ALLOW_ALL_ORIGINS` is not an option: wildcard-with-credentials is rejected by
browsers and would loosen the authenticated API. Instead, exclude this path from
`django-cors-headers` and set the headers inside the view, never sending
`Access-Control-Allow-Credentials`:

```python
CORS_URLS_REGEX = r'^(?!/api/widget/bootstrap/).*$'
```

### 4. Console API (authenticated)

All routes under `/api/widget/`, using `RolePermission` with
`@require_roles('ADMIN', 'MANAGER')`, scoped to `request.platform_user.company`. A
company id is never accepted from the client.

Every route now hangs off a subscription id, because a company holds one per domain.
`<sid>` is always validated against `request.platform_user.company` — a subscription
id is never trusted on its own.

| Method | Path | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/api/widget/subscriptions/` | every domain the company holds, with status, plan, and pages measured |
| POST | `/api/widget/subscriptions/` | claim a domain; checks the trial ledger (§1) before granting a trial |
| GET | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/` | status, plan, interval, period dates, trial end, scheduled change |
| GET | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/verification/` | DNS TXT token and current status |
| GET / PUT | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/config/` | current draft, save draft (validated) |
| POST | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/config/publish/` | draft → new `WidgetConfigVersion`, bump `current_version` |
| POST | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/config/rollback/` | `{version: n}` → republish an earlier payload |
| GET / POST | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/keys/` | list; issue new (cap active keys, e.g. 5) |
| DELETE | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/keys/<id>/` | revoke |
| GET | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/usage/` | pages measured over time, denial reasons |
| POST | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/change-plan/` | `{plan_code, interval}` → apply (§2.4) |
| POST | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/change-plan/preview/` | proration, effective date, warnings — **no side effects** |
| POST | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/cancel/` | sets `cancel_at_period_end` |
| POST | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/reactivate/` | clears `cancel_at_period_end` before period end |
| POST | `/api/widget/subscriptions/<sid>/auto-renew/` | `{enabled: bool}` |
| GET | `/api/widget/plans/` | public plan catalog |
| GET | `/api/widget/invoices/` | all widget invoices for the company, `kind=WIDGET_SUBSCRIPTION`, tagged by domain |
| POST | `/api/widget/quote-request/` | Managed-tier enquiry (§2.9); also reachable unauthenticated from the pricing page |

`POST /api/widget/subscriptions/` is the self-serve entry point and carries the trial
constraint. It resolves the eTLD+1 of the submitted hostname, looks up
`WidgetDomainTrial`, and either starts a 7-day trial or responds
`409 {"error": "trial_already_used", "registrable_domain": "acme.com"}` — offering a
paid start rather than a trial. Two requests racing for the same domain are resolved
by the unique index on `registrable_domain`, not by a read-then-write check.

Invoices remain a company-level list rather than per-subscription: a customer with
six domains wants one billing page, with each line attributed to its domain.

`widget_enabled` is added to `build_user_payload()` in `apps/api/utils.py` — true
when the company holds at least one subscription in any status — so the dashboard can
hide the navigation entry without a probe request.

The **preview endpoint is not optional**. A plan change carries a proration charge, a
future effective date, and possible entitlement conflicts; a customer must see all
three before confirming, and the confirm call must be a separate request. Both share
one service function so the preview cannot drift from what actually happens.

Plan changes are `ADMIN`-only — `MANAGER` may edit config but not alter what the
company is billed.

### 5. Django Admin — granting and billing operations

`apps/widget/admin.py` follows the conventions in `apps/core/admin.py`.

- `WidgetSubscriptionAdmin` —
  `list_display = ('hostname', 'company', 'plan', 'status', 'billing_interval', 'current_period_end', 'auto_renew', 'pages_this_period')`,
  `list_filter = ('status', 'plan', 'billing_interval', 'auto_renew', 'verification_status')`,
  `search_fields = ('hostname', 'company__name', 'company__slug')`,
  with a `WidgetKey` inline and readonly audit fields
  (`granted_by`, `granted_at`, period dates).
- Admin actions: `grant_trial`, `grant_access` (plan + interval chosen in an
  intermediate form), `change_plan`, `suspend`, `revoke`, `issue_new_key`,
  `extend_grace`, `force_renew_now`, and `override_trial_ledger` (§1 — for a domain
  that genuinely changed hands, requiring a typed reason). Each writes a
  `TelemetryEvent` through the existing `log_telemetry()` helper.
- `WidgetDomainTrialAdmin` — searchable by `registrable_domain`, readonly except for
  the staff override. Support will need it the first time a customer reports that a
  trial is already used on a domain they have never signed up with.
- `WidgetPlanAdmin` — effective-dated, with the same "new row, not in-place edit"
  guard used for pricing: editing `monthly_price` / `annual_price` on a plan that
  has live subscriptions is refused, with a message pointing at plan versioning.
- `WidgetSubscriptionChangeAdmin` and `WidgetUsagePeriodAdmin` are fully readonly —
  audit surfaces, not editing surfaces.
- The full key is shown in the detail view with a copy affordance. It is not a
  secret, and support needs to read it back to customers.

`grant_access` runs in one transaction and creates the `WidgetSubscription` (in
`TRIALING` when the plan has `trial_days`, otherwise `ACTIVE` with a period opened),
one `WidgetKey`, an empty `WidgetConfiguration`, a `WidgetSubscriptionChange`
audit row, and a published `WidgetConfigVersion(version=1)` built from plan defaults
— so a freshly issued key works immediately instead of returning `403` until someone
opens the dashboard.

Staff must never edit subscription status directly to move money-adjacent state.
Status transitions go through the lifecycle service in `apps/widget/services/`, the
same rule `JobCharge.save()` already enforces for billing lifecycle in
`apps/core/models.py`.

### 6. Caching, Telemetry, and Load

Bootstrap is the highest-volume endpoint the platform will own: one request per page
view across every customer site.

**Read path.** Cache the resolved `(key, host) → payload` for 5 minutes under
`widget:bootstrap:{key}:{host}` (Redis in production, locmem in development).
Invalidate on publish, key revocation, domain change, **and every subscription state
transition** (plan change, suspension, renewal, period rollover) by bumping a
per-subscription cache generation counter rather than enumerating keys. Cache denials
for unknown keys too, so a key-scanning bot does not reach the database on every
attempt.

Because entitlements are applied at read (§2.5), a stale cache is the only thing
standing between a downgrade and its enforcement — hence the 5-minute ceiling and the
generation bump on transition, rather than relying on expiry alone.

**Write path.** Do not `UPDATE` `last_seen_at` or a counter per request; that turns a
cached read into a write per page view and will not survive real traffic. Instead:

- Emit a `TelemetryEvent` (`widget.bootstrap_allowed` / `widget.bootstrap_denied`)
  **sampled** — 1-in-50, or only on cache miss — with
  `metadata = {key_id, host, reason, version}`.
- Update `last_seen_at` at most once per hour per key, guarded by a cache flag.
- Derive the usage graph from a nightly rollup, not from live Postgres counters.

**Distinct page counting is the exception, and it is unsampled.** As set out in §2.6,
sampling destroys a distinct count. Every bootstrap therefore performs two Redis
operations — `PFADD` into the period's HyperLogLog, and a set-membership check that
enqueues a new `path_hash` for the nightly flush — and no Postgres write. Redis is
sized for this; Postgres is not. If Redis is unavailable, counting degrades (the
period is flagged `measurement_degraded`) but serving is never affected: a failure in
the metering path must never take the widget down.

That the count feeds an upsell conversation rather than an invoice line is what makes
~0.8% HLL error acceptable here. Had the pricing metered traffic, this would need to
be exact and auditable, and the design would be considerably heavier — a further
argument for the site-size model.

**Throttling.** DRF `AnonRateThrottle` scoped to the bootstrap view and keyed on
`key + client IP`, set generously (around 120/min). Its job is to blunt abuse of an
unknown key, not to shape legitimate traffic.

### 7. Dashboard Page

Because a company holds one subscription per domain, this is **two pages**, following
the `projects.html` / `project-details.html` pattern already in the frontend rather
than the single-page `settings.html` one:

- `widget.html` — the domain list: hostname, plan, status, trial countdown, pages
  measured against `max_pages`, and an "Add a domain" action that runs the trial-ledger
  check from §4.
- `widget-details.html` — everything for one domain.

Both follow the conventions in `js/settings.js`: `DOMContentLoaded` → `requireAuth()`
→ `apiRequest()`. The navigation entry in `js/navigation.js` renders only when
`user.widget_enabled`.

The detail page carries:

1. **Install** — generated snippet with a copy button and a key selector:
   `<script src="https://cdn.wcagify.ai/wcagify.min.js" data-key="wcg_…" defer></script>`
2. **Domain** — the licensed hostname and its verification status. Not editable: a
   subscription's domain is what was bought. Changing site is a new subscription, and
   the UI should say so rather than offering an edit field that cannot work.
3. **Appearance & features** — accent color, position, icon, profile and tool
   toggles, with a live preview iframe loading the real `wcagify.min.js` against the
   draft config.
4. **Publish bar** — unpublished-changes indicator, Publish, Discard, and version
   history with rollback.
5. **Plan & billing** — this domain's plan, interval, renewal date, trial countdown,
   measured pages against `max_pages`, the five-tier comparison with
   upgrade/downgrade, the proration preview dialog, monthly/annual toggle, auto-renew,
   cancel/reactivate, and the "Ask for a quote" form for `managed`. Invoices are
   company-wide and live on the existing billing page, tagged by domain — a customer
   with six domains should not visit six pages to see what they paid.

Two states this page must handle explicitly rather than by omission: a **trial with N
days left** (persistent banner with a convert call to action), and **`PAST_DUE` inside
grace** (a banner stating the widget is still live and the exact date it stops). The
second is the one a customer will judge the product on — if the first they hear of a
lapsed subscription is the widget vanishing from their site, the grace period bought
nothing.

Two more states the domain list must render honestly: a **trial that has expired**
(the widget has stopped; offer a paid start, and do not offer another trial, because
the ledger will refuse it), and a **domain claimed but never installed** — no
bootstrap seen yet — which is the moment most self-serve signups stall.

A third state: **over the page limit**. It is not an error and must not read
like one — the widget is working normally. The correct tone is "your site has grown to
4 200 pages; Large covers up to 10 000", with an upgrade button. Never a red banner,
never a warning icon.

Features not included in the company's plan are shown **disabled with the tier that
unlocks them**, not hidden. Under the current catalog this applies only to `managed`'s
expert remediation, since the self-serve tiers ship an identical widget.

### 8. Contract for the Widget Repo

Implemented separately in `wcagify-frontend.ai`; recorded here so both sides agree.

- Read `data-key` from the script tag, alongside the existing `data-root` and friends.
- **No key preserves today's behavior**: self-hosted mode using local
  `WcagifyConfig`, so `demo.html` and existing embeds keep working.
- With a key, call
  `fetch(BOOTSTRAP_URL + '?key=' + key, { credentials: 'omit', cache: 'default' })`
  before init. Any non-`200` warns once to the console, renders nothing, and does not
  retry.
- Merge order must be documented and fixed. Recommended: server config wins for
  tenant-scoped settings (accent, position, profiles, tools); local `data-*` wins for
  structural selectors (`data-root`, `data-main`), since only the site author knows
  their DOM.
- Cache the payload in `sessionStorage` keyed by version so an SPA does not refetch
  on every route change.
- The widget README currently promises "nothing leaves the browser." A bootstrap
  call is a genuine external request. That wording must be narrowed — for example,
  "no page content, no user data, no identifiers; one configuration fetch on load" —
  before a customer privacy review encounters the discrepancy.

## Build Order

Sequenced so that each step is independently shippable and the commercial layer lands
only once the product layer works.

1. `apps.widget` models — including `WidgetPlan`, per-domain `WidgetSubscription`
   with its partial unique index, `WidgetDomainTrial`, `WidgetSubscriptionChange`,
   and `WidgetUsagePeriod` — migration `0001_initial`, and admin with
   grant/suspend/revoke. The full schema goes in from the start even
   though the lifecycle engine comes later; adding money-adjacent columns to a table
   that already has live subscriptions is the expensive version of this.
2. `apps/api/widget_public.py` bootstrap, `CORS_URLS_REGEX`, throttle, and tests
   (allowed, unknown key, wrong origin, missing `Origin`, `www.` equivalence,
   subdomain **rejected**, revoked key, `If-None-Match`), with the entitlement filter
   applied at read.
3. Caching, generation-counter invalidation, and sampled telemetry.
4. Console API — config, keys, domains — plus `widget_enabled` in the user payload.
5. `widget.html` dashboard: install, domains, appearance, publish.
6. Plan catalog seeded (`seed_widget_plans`) with the five tiers from §2.1, plan tab
   in the dashboard, quote-request flow for `managed`.
7. Page measurement: Redis HLL and path-hash queue on the bootstrap path, nightly
   flush to `WidgetPageObservation` / `WidgetUsagePeriod`, usage display, 80%/100%
   notifications.
8. Lifecycle engine: `renew_widget_subscriptions` command, 7-day trials auto-expiring,
   the trial-ledger check on claim, grace and suspension, `CreditBalanceProvider`.
9. Invoicing: `Invoice.kind`, `WidgetInvoiceLine`, invoice list in the dashboard,
   dunning emails.
10. Upgrade/downgrade with proration and scheduled changes.
11. Domain verification.
12. Payment gateway — a third `PaymentProvider` implementation plus a webhook
    endpoint. No changes to steps 8–10.

Steps 1–5 deliver a working, staff-granted widget with no commerce. That is a
legitimate place to stop and get customers using it while the plan design is settled.

## Decisions That Are Expensive to Reverse

- **Origin-header authorization, not a query parameter** (§3). Changing this later
  means every embedded script tag on every customer site is already trusting the
  wrong thing.
- **No database write per bootstrap request** (§6). Retrofitting this after launch
  means doing it under production load.
- **Entitlements live on the plan and are applied at read** (§1, §2.5). If they are
  copied onto the subscription or baked into the published payload, every plan change
  and downgrade silently fails to take effect, and discovering that requires auditing
  live traffic rather than reading code.
- **Plans are effective-dated, never edited in place** (§2.1). Editing a live plan's
  price reprices every existing subscriber at their next renewal with no record of
  what they had agreed to.
- **One invoice ledger shared with job billing** (§2.7). Splitting widget invoices
  into a separate table is easy now and a data migration with customer-visible
  numbering consequences later.
- **Pages are the meter; traffic is never metered** (§2.1, §2.6). The schema
  deliberately cannot express a pageview charge, because "Unlimited pageviews &
  visits" is a promise printed on every pricing card. Adding those columns later
  should require this document to change first.
- **Exceeding the page limit never stops the widget serving** (§2.6). The failure
  mode being avoided is removing an accessibility affordance from disabled visitors
  on a live public site over a billing threshold.
- **One subscription is one domain** (§1). Every route, every config, every invoice
  line, and the whole dashboard hang off this. Moving to multi-domain subscriptions
  later is a data migration plus a rewrite of §4 and §7 — moving the other way is
  worse. This is the single most structural decision in the document.
- **The trial ledger is keyed on eTLD+1 and rows are never deleted** (§1). Keying on
  the exact hostname makes the one-trial rule trivially bypassable via subdomains;
  deleting rows on cancellation reopens it for the whole domain.

Everything else in this document can be changed later without a painful migration.

## Status of features in this module

| Feature | Sub-feature | Status | Notes |
|---------|-------------|--------|-------|
| Plan Catalog | Five tiers: Small/Medium/Large/Huge/Managed | PENDING | Priced by site size; seeded via `seed_widget_plans` |
| Plan Catalog | Monthly + annual prices, both absolute columns | PENDING | $39/$390, $49/$490, $109/$1090, $169/$1690 |
| Data Model | `WidgetSubscription` (one per domain) + `WidgetKey` | PENDING | New app `apps.widget`; partial unique index on `hostname` |
| Data Model | `WidgetDomainTrial` ledger | PENDING | eTLD+1 keyed, never deleted; needs `publicsuffix2` |
| Data Model | `WidgetConfiguration` + `WidgetConfigVersion` | PENDING | Immutable versions, mirrors `JobPricingSnapshot` |
| Data Model | `WidgetPlan` effective-dated catalog | PENDING | Entitlements + pricing; new row per price change |
| Data Model | `WidgetSubscriptionChange` + `WidgetUsagePeriod` | PENDING | Immutable audit; scheduling for deferred downgrades |
| Bootstrap API | `GET /api/widget/bootstrap/` + origin authorization | PENDING | `AllowAny`; `CORS_URLS_REGEX` exclusion required |
| Bootstrap API | ETag / `Cache-Control` / `304` handling | PENDING | Etag sourced from `WidgetConfigVersion` |
| Caching & Telemetry | Cached resolution + sampled events + hourly `last_seen_at` | PENDING | No write per request |
| Caching & Telemetry | Nightly rollup: HLL flush + usage graphs | PENDING | Reads `telemetry.telemetry_events` + Redis |
| Console API | Config draft / publish / rollback | PENDING | `@require_roles('ADMIN', 'MANAGER')` |
| Console API | Subscription list + domain claim with trial check | PENDING | `409 trial_already_used`; race resolved by unique index |
| Console API | Key CRUD per subscription | PENDING | Active-key cap per domain |
| Console API | Plan change + proration preview, cancel, auto-renew | PENDING | `ADMIN`-only; preview and apply share one service |
| Subscription | 7-day trial, no card, auto-expiry | PENDING | One trial per registrable domain, ever |
| Subscription | Monthly / annual intervals | PENDING | Absolute annual price, not a computed discount |
| Subscription | `renew_widget_subscriptions` daily command | PENDING | Idempotent, row-locked, deterministic idempotency key |
| Subscription | Grace period + dunning + suspension | PENDING | `PAST_DUE` keeps serving; only lapse stops the widget |
| Subscription | Upgrade (immediate, prorated) / downgrade (deferred) | PENDING | Direction from `WidgetPlan.rank`, never price |
| Subscription | Downgrade below measured pages warns, never blocks | PENDING | No domain-count conflict exists in this model |
| Entitlements | Plan filter applied at bootstrap read | PENDING | Not baked into the published payload |
| Page Metering | Redis HLL + `WidgetPageObservation`, unsampled | PENDING | Salted path hashes only; degrades without affecting serving |
| Page Metering | 80%/100% notifications + upgrade prompt | PENDING | `NOTIFY` policy: never stops serving |
| Managed Tier | `WidgetQuoteRequest` + admin assignment flow | PENDING | Quote-only; non-public per-company plan row |
| Invoicing | `Invoice.kind` + `WidgetInvoiceLine` | PENDING | Reuses `apps.core.Invoice`; lines must sum to total |
| Invoicing | Currency invariant compliance | PENDING | Per `prds/billing-currency-invariant.md`; prefer per-currency plans |
| Payments | `PaymentProvider` interface + credit/manual implementations | PENDING | Gateway is a later third implementation |
| Payments | Gateway integration + webhooks | DEFERRED | Explicitly out of the first delivery |
| Django Admin | Grant / suspend / revoke actions + inlines | PENDING | Grant is one transaction, publishes v1 |
| Django Admin | Plan admin, change/grace/force-renew actions, readonly audits | PENDING | Status transitions only via lifecycle service |
| Dashboard | `widget.html` domain list + `widget-details.html` | PENDING | Follows `projects` / `project-details` pattern |
| Dashboard | Live preview iframe | PENDING | Loads real `wcagify.min.js` with draft config |
| Dashboard | Plan & billing tab, trial/grace/over-limit banners, invoices | PENDING | Over-limit reads as an upsell, not an error |
| Domain Verification | DNS TXT flow | PENDING | Matters more under global hostname uniqueness |
| Widget Repo | `data-key` + bootstrap fetch + merge order | PENDING | Implemented in `wcagify-frontend.ai` |
| Widget Repo | README privacy wording update | PENDING | Bootstrap call contradicts current claim |
